 "open_notes": [
  {
   "id": "N1",
   "from": "Fundamentals Analyst",
   "to": "Market Analyst",
   "section": "market",
   "note": "The deck's $2.4B serviceable market is 13,200 sites x 2 analyzers x 8 panels a day x 300 days x $38 = $2.41B, but 8 panels a day is 7.9x the 30.5 cartridges per analyzer a month (1.0 a day) its own 55 evaluation analyzers record. At that observed rate the same site count yields $367m at $38 or $323m at the $33.40 realised price. The distributor LOI covers 300 sites, is non-binding, and sits against 41 sites today.",
   "seen": true
  },
  {
   "id": "N2",
   "from": "Fundamentals Analyst",
   "to": "Valuation Analyst",
   "section": "funding_valuation",
   "note": "The $140m pre-money is fully diluted and already contains the bridge conversion and a 10% post-money option pool: 2,571,392 B-1 shares at $2.4423 = $6.28m, consistent with $6.0m plus 8% accrued to the 2027-01-29 close. Post-money is 52,204,508 shares x $3.2564 = $170.0m, so the effective pre-money for existing holders is $133.7m. On my cash model a further $10-15m is needed in 2028, which argues for pricing below the ask.",
   "seen": true
  },
  {
   "id": "N3",
   "from": "Fundamentals Analyst",
   "to": "Red Team Analyst",
   "section": "risk",
   "note": "Two facts for the risk section. (1) The deck's 510(k) number K261734 is, in the public device record, the Klassic Infection Spacer System by Total Joint Orthopedics, an orthopedic device, so the clearance date behind every 2027 revenue line is unverified; the 2025 average 510(k) took 146 days to clearance. (2) At $6.9m cash and $1.15m monthly net burn, runway ends in March 2027, and the round's target close is 2027-01-29.",
   "seen": false
  },
  {
   "id": "N5",
   "from": "Regulatory Analyst",
   "to": "Fundamentals Analyst",
   "section": "financials",
   "note": "Reimbursement caps the launch: 2026 CLFS pays $12.47 (CPT 84484) + $9.71 (83036) = $22.18 for the two assays in the first 510(k), against a $38 list / $33.40 realised cartridge; the $39.26 NT-proBNP code (83880) sits in the second submission [S119, S134]. CLIA waiver is planned Q3 2027 after clearance, so no untrained-staff use before 2028, yet the 300-site/end-2027 and FY2028 $41.0M plan assume it [S136, S133].",
   "seen": false
  },
  {
   "id": "N6",
   "from": "Regulatory Analyst",
   "to": "Market Analyst",
   "section": "market",
   "note": "Pre-waiver the channel is only sites holding a moderate-complexity CLIA certificate and qualified staff (company-admitted, deck p.5) [S136]; a waiver decision lands in late 2027 or 2028 on the Q3 2027 filing plan. That caps the 13,200-site SAM until then. Note also 8 panels/day per analyzer in the model against 41 cartridges per site per month actual (1,680 cartridges across 41 sites), roughly a sixfold gap [S1].",
   "seen": true
  },
  {
   "id": "N8",
   "from": "Product Analyst",
   "to": "Fundamentals Analyst",
   "section": "financials",
   "note": "Two reconciliation points for the financials section from the product side. (1) FY2025 evaluation-programme revenue of $1.1M does not reconcile with 55 analyzers x $650 x 12 months = $429k at the site table on deck p.7; the gap is unexplained. (2) FY2026E gross margin of 61% (deck p.10) cannot come from cartridges at $33.40 realised against $16.20 cost (51.5%, deck p.9), so roughly ten points of margin must come from evaluation fees or contract development, which do not scale with cartridge volume. Both from S1.",
   "seen": false
  },
  {
   "id": "N9",
   "from": "Product Analyst",
   "to": "Market Analyst",
   "section": "competition",
   "note": "Three facts for the competitive section. Abbott cleared an i-STAT hs-TnI cartridge on the Alinity system on 2026-06-11 (K253051, S25), so a point-of-care high-sensitivity troponin is already on the market. i-STAT chemistry cartridges are already cleared for capillary whole blood (S137), which weakens the deck's venous-versus-finger-stick differentiation. And no troponin assay appears among the roughly 60 CLIA waiver-by-application decisions FDA published 2019-2026 (S109); waived chemistry is single-analyte (potassium, HbA1c, glucose, UACR).",
   "seen": true
  },
  {
   "id": "N10",
   "from": "Product Analyst",
   "to": "Valuation Analyst",
   "section": "funding_valuation",
   "note": "Valuation anchor from the product side: Roche paid an aggregate $353.5M at closing on 2024-07-29 for LumiraDx's multi-assay point-of-care platform, including $62.6M to repay the senior lender (S114, S112). That is a market price for a developed multi-assay platform whose menu was already cleared/CE-marked, sold out of distress - useful against the $140M pre-money for a company with two of five assays under review and the platform still at one pilot line.",
   "seen": true
  },
  {
   "id": "N11",
   "from": "Company Analyst",
   "to": "Red Team Analyst",
   "section": "risk",
   "note": "Two facts for the risk section from section 1: (1) the deck's 510(k) number K261734 resolves in the public device record to the Klassic Infection Spacer System by Total Joint Orthopedics, an orthopaedic implant; pending submissions are not published, so this is not proof of no filing, but the filing date and the 2026-08-21 AI request rest on company assertion alone [S118, S121]. (2) On a sale, Corvia's 40% minority (CTO Lindqvist holds 22% of Corvia) may put its stake to Vantelle at fair market value, and the CM-1 cartridge patent sits in Corvia, not the issuer [S1].",
   "seen": false
  },
  {
   "id": "N12",
   "from": "Company Analyst",
   "to": "Valuation Analyst",
   "section": "funding_valuation",
   "note": "Cap table re-check for your round pricing: the $140M pre-money divides by 42,991,881 FD pre-round shares to $3.2564 ($170.0M post). Only $118.5M attaches to the 36.4M existing shares; the other $21.5M is 2,571,392 bridge shares ($8.4M, 75% of price = $2.4423) plus 4,020,489 pool-top-up shares ($13.1M) inside the pre-money. Series A price was $2.9032 ($36.0M / 12.4M), so +12.2% in 35-56 months. Preference $76.78M + $5.0M loan; because Series A is 1x participating, the $30M Series B returns its capital only near a $209M exit (0.70x at $170M).",
   "seen": false
  },
  {
   "id": "N13",
   "from": "Company Analyst",
   "to": "Product Analyst",
   "section": "technology_product",
   "note": "IP facts for the technology section: CM-1 (US 15/707,915, cartridge sealing film and reagent blister, granted 2021-01) is owned by Corvia Microfluidics LLC, which Vantelle holds at 60%; the CTO holds 22% of Corvia and Vantelle pays it 3% of cartridge net sales, so the enabling consumable patent is not wholly owned and the CTO earns 0.66% of cartridge revenue outside his equity [S1]. VD-2 (five-assay optical reader, US 17/662,019) took a final rejection in 2026-07 with an appeal undecided [S1].",
   "seen": false
  }
 ],
