{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"red_team_review (by Red Team Analyst):
{
 "answers": {
  "most_damaging_fact": "No FDA filing. If the 2026-05-18 510(k) was never submitted, every 2027-2029 revenue line, the $2.4M stocking order, the 300-site letter of intent and the 2027-01-29 close lose their basis, and the residual is a pilot line plus a 128-patient single-site study worth 0.1-0.2x, not the 0.77x the preference now implies after Corvia's claim. The damage is concentrated because the same unverified filing sits under the whole plan and the $140M price. The corroborating gap is that no FDA receipt, acknowledgment or information letter has been produced, and the number in the deck belongs to another company's cleared device [S185, S118, S1].",
  "company_only_claims": "The capital structure and terms - $140M pre-money, $170M post-money, 43.0M fully diluted shares, the $76.78M preference stack, the 1.25x preference and ratchet; the $6.9M cash, $1.15M monthly burn, $1.375M plan loss, $8M insider commitment and $5.0M Bluestem loan; FY2025 revenue $2.6M, FY2026E $5.9M and the $2.4M stocking order; 41 sites, 55 analyzers and 1,680 cartridges a month; the 92% reorder rate; the $33.40 realised price and $16.20 cartridge cost; the 128-patient study with 11.8% CV; the 300-site letter of intent; and the Corvia 60/40 terms, 3% royalty and 40% put. Independent sources verify the FDA record, fee-schedule rates, site counts and peer transactions, not the company's own numbers [S1, S185, S119, S134].",
  "generic_section": "No section is a generic category essay, but competition is the one whose central premise rests on absence. It asserts a unique waived cardiac gap while conceding 'whether an older waived cardiac assay exists is unsettled' and marking QuidelOrtho Triage's waiver status 'not confirmed' [S5, S109]. The check's 'no economic/strategic interpretation' flag is a false negative - the razor-and-blade and GPO-rebate mechanism is explicit - but the waiver premise needs a positive search of pre-2019 waiver decisions and Triage's current CLIA status before three sections treat 'no waived cardiac panel' as fact. Financials has adopted the published CY2027 cut ($18.85 against $14.55, a $43-49M funding need, FY2028 $34.9M), so the remaining gap is market's $303.7M, which uses list price and evaluation-site utilisation with commercial collections undisclosed [S188, S1].",
  "unpriced_risk": "The Corvia 40% fair-value put and the 3% royalty are now deducted in the valuation waterfall - 6.6% of exit value, $9.2M at $140M and $14.9M at $225M ahead of Meridian - taking the $140M expected return from 1.18x after the claim and before the $18-23M Series C to 1.0x after it (0.9x at the $20M top of the range), and the $225M base from 1.11x to 1.03x. The risk section prices that; the recommendation does not make the put bought out, capped or subordinated a condition of the lead. The same round dilutes Meridian 11.4-14.1% and is inside the return table; the bear is 0.77x.",
  "first_challenge": "The first challenge is why Meridian would price any 2027 revenue before seeing FDA's own correspondence. The only 510(k) number in the deck, K261734, is FDA's record for Total Joint Orthopedics' Klassic Infection Spacer System, received 2026-05-26 and cleared 2026-07-23 [S185]; FDA does not publish pending submissions and neither an acknowledgment nor the 2026-08-21 information letter has been produced, so the filing is company assertion. A committee would make those letters a condition precedent and hold the price at $100M until they arrive. The second challenge is the security: at $140M the Series B returns 1.0x to a $206M exit before the $18-23M Series C, $216M if it ranks pari passu and $251M if senior, so the 1.25x preference and ratchet lift the floor and mid case but cannot convert a 1.0x expected return into a venture return; only price does that. The third is the exit: Werfen is the only coherent strategic and has completed no acquisition in 2026, Abbott already owns i-STAT and Roche owns LumiraDx, so a pre-clearance buyer is a story, not a base case.",
  "downside_survival": "At $140M the conclusion survives only as capital preservation, not as a venture return. A market 50% smaller ($152M) leaves 300 sites at 15% share but cuts revenue; revenue 30% lower on the cut plan takes FY2028 to $24.4M and the exit to $98-122M at 4-5x, 1.0x; CAC 2x doubles the cost of the 22-a-month site ramp; financing six months late is insolvency, because $6.9M at $1.15M a month ends 2027-03-30 and the round is due 2027-01-29 [S1]. The $303.7M SAM is itself a ceiling: at $33.40 realised it is $267M, and it uses 41 cartridges a site a month from 41 evaluation sites, 60% on free analyzers, with commercial collections undisclosed; PAMA's private-payor median is below Medicare for 1,171 codes [S188]. The 1.25x preference and $6M clearance tranche return 0.77x in the bear and 1.03x at the $225M base; the 2.18x bull carries the 1.18x after the Corvia claim and before the $18-23M Series C, 1.0x after it. The 30% weight is defensible for the clearance-failure path but not for the 10-15% no-filing branch at 0.1-0.2x, which is not in the $30M bear; with it the effective downside is nearer 40% and the expected return at $140M falls below 1.0x. At $100M full delivery returns 2.55x. The structure survives the downside; the price does not earn the upside.",
  "ocr_claims": "There are no OCR-derived claims in the report. The client deck is a digital PDF read as text; its page 1 instruction to automated diligence tools is quoted as a disclosure finding, not acted on [S1]. Every company figure is labelled unverified; FDA records, fee schedules and peer filings are cited from primary or independent sources [S185, S119, S181]."
 },
